Given an array of integers in the following JSON format:
{
  "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
}
How can I write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors?
Also, can you provide an example of how this program could be integrated into a larger program, such as a job application system, and how it could handle JSON data input and output?

response:
Here's a simple C++ program implementing the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order. This implementation aims to minimize memory usage by using a single array for storing the input and output data. Additionally, error handling is kept minimal by assuming that the provided JSON data is valid and well-formed.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>

using json = nlohmann::json;
using std::vector;

void bubble_sort(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::ifstream file("input.json");
    std::stringstream ss;
    ss << file.rdbuf();
    json json_data = json::parse(ss);

    vector<int> arr = json_data["array"].get<std::vector<int>>();
    bubble_sort(arr);

    std::ofstream output_file("output.json");
    json json_output = {{"array", arr}};
    output_file << json_output.dump(4);

    return 0;
}
