Here's an example of how you can create a React hook to convert between different temperature units using the `useState` hook:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const useTemperatureConverter = (initialValue) => {
  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState(initialValue);
  const [unit, setUnit] = useState('celsius');

  const handleConvert = () => {
    if (unit === 'celsius') {
      setTemperature((temperature * 9 / 5) + 32);
      setUnit('fahrenheit');
    } else {
      setTemperature((temperature - 32) * 5 / 9);
      setUnit('celsius');
    }
  };

  return { temperature, unit, handleConvert };
};

export default useTemperatureConverter;
```
You can use this hook in your components like this:
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { useTemperatureConverter } from './useTemperatureConverter';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  const { temperature, unit, handleConvert } = useTemperatureConverter(30);

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{temperature}°{unit}</h1>
      <button onClick={handleConvert}>Convert</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

To achieve this effect, you'll need to use PHP to get the current time and then use CSS to style the sun accordingly. Here's an example of how you can do this:

1. Create a PHP file (e.g., `sun_styler.php`) with the following code:
```php
