To make the planets appear less crowded, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function to ensure a minimum distance between each planet. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this updated version, we added a `minDistance` parameter to the function, which specifies the minimum distance between two planets. If a planet is too close to an existing planet, it will not be added to the list of planets. You can adjust the value of `minDistance` to control the spacing between the planets.

For example, if you want to ensure that there is at least 5 units of distance between each planet, you can call the function like this:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 5));
```

This will generate a list of 5 planets with a minimum distance of 5 units between each planet.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows (words or phrases that appear at the end of a line without proper justification) from a given text. This utility uses the `wordWrap` and `lineClamp` CSS properties to achieve this.

```javascript
import React, { useRef } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const textRef = useRef(null);

  const handleResize = () => {
    const textNode = textRef.current;
    if (textNode) {
      const width = textNode.offsetWidth;
      const lines = textNode.textContent.split('\n');

      for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
        const line = lines[i];
        const words = line.split(' ');

        for (let j = 0; j < words.length; j++) {
          const word = words[j];
          const wordWidth = textNode.offsetWidth - 10; // Adjust the padding as needed

          if (wordWidth < word.length * 8) { // Assuming each character takes up 8 pixels in width
            words.splice(j, 1);
            j--;
          }
        }

        lines[i] = words.join(' ');
      }

      textNode.textContent = lines.join('\n');
    }
  };

  return (
    <div ref={textRef} onResize={handleResize}>
      {text}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from:

```javascript
<RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed non risus. Suspendisse lectus tortor, dignissim sit amet, adipiscing nec, ultricies sed, dolor. Cras mattis consectetur purus, sed tincidunt magna aliquet non." />
```

